  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 322 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper level low will be anchored to our northeast \ntoday with a weak shortwave moving through the longwave trough. This \nenergy is expected to spark isolated showers this afternoon but the \nlack of any appreciable instability or moisture will keep the areal \ncoverage low and the intensity light. We will see an increase in \nshower activity across the region on Saturday as a second shortwave \nhelps to deepen the low to our northeast and the low sinks to the \nsoutheast. This will increase mid-level lift while increasing mid-\nlevel lapse rates and therefore slightly increase instability. With \neast/northeast winds in the low to mid levels, we should advect in \nsome Atlantic moisture which will also aid in increasing the areal \ncoverage of showers. However, precipitation rates will remain meager \nwith just some periodic light rain throughout the day on Saturday. \nDeep layer ridging will build overhead on Sunday and continue into \nMonday which will keep seasonably cool temperatures in the forecast \nwhile also bringing plentiful sunshine and dry weather.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: After a seasonable and dry holiday weekend, chances for \nshowers look to return throughout next week, although no significant \nweather is expected at this time. Starting with Monday night into \nTuesday, a cold front looks to push across the region driving shower \nactivity, although rainfall will be limited given lack of deep \nmoisture. As the week progresses, several shortwaves look to move \nthrough, bringing chances for light rain showers each day, with \nThursday possibly seeing the most coverage. Despite the chances for \nshowers, no day looks to be a complete washout at this time given \nthe showery nature of the precipitation, but an extended period of \ndry weather is not expected either. Temperatures for next week look \nto be on the cool side for the most part, with high temperatures in \nthe upper 60s and 70s through the week and overnight lows in the 40s \nand 50s.",
